      "text": "To add a description below a table in LaTeX, there are several ways depending on how you want it to look. \n\nHere are the three most common methods, ranging from the \"quick and easy\" to the \"professional\" way.\n\n---\n\n### 1. The Professional Way (`threeparttable`)\nThis is the most popular method because it automatically wraps the text to the exact width of your table, so the description doesn\u0027t spill out past the edges of the columns.\n\n**Requires:** `\\usepackage{threeparttable}`\n\n```latex\n\\begin{table}[ht]\n\\centering\n\\begin{threeparttable}\n    \\caption{This is the Title}\n    \\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}\n        \\hline\n        Header 1 \u0026 Header 2 \u0026 Header 3 \\\\ \\hline\n        Data A   \u0026 Data B   \u0026 Data C   \\\\ \\hline\n    \\end{tabular}\n    \\begin{tablenotes}\n      \\small\n      \\item \\textbf{Note:} Here are the few sentences you want to add below your table. You can describe your data sources, symbols used, or statistical significance here.\n    \\end{tablenotes}\n\\end{threeparttable}\n\\end{table}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 2. The Simple Way (Manual Text)\nIf you don\u0027t want to use extra packages, you can simply place text immediately after the `tabular` environment. Use `\\small` or `\\footnotesize` to distinguish it from the main body text.\n\n```latex\n\\begin{table}[ht]\n\\centering\n\\caption{This is the Title}\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    Header 1 \u0026 Header 2 \u0026 Header 3 \\\\ \\hline\n    Data A   \u0026 Data B   \u0026 Data C   \\\\ \\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\n\\vspace{5pt} % Adds a small gap between the table and the text\n\\begin{flushleft}\n    \\small \\textbf{Description:} This is a manual description placed below the table. Note that this text will use the full width of the page margin unless you wrap it in a minipage.\n\\end{flushleft}\n\\end{table}\n```\n\n---\n\n### 3. The `caption` Package Way (`\\caption*`)\nIf you want the description to look like a caption but without the \"Table 1:\" prefix, you can use the `caption` package.\n\n**Requires:** `\\usepackage{caption}`\n\n```latex\n\\begin{table}[ht]\n\\centering\n\\caption{Main Table Title}\n\\begin{tabular}{|c|c|c|}\n    \\hline\n    Header 1 \u0026 Header 2 \\\\ \\hline\n    Data A   \u0026 Data B   \\\\ \\hline\n\\end{tabular}\n\n\\caption*{ \\textit{Note:} This is an unnumbered caption that acts as a description. It will automatically center itself and use caption styling.}\n\\end{table}\n```\n\n---\n\n### Which one should you choose?\n\n1.  **Use `threeparttable`** if you want the description to align perfectly with the left and right edges of your table (Standard for academic papers).\n2.  **Use Manual Text** if you want the description to be as wide as the page margins.\n3.  **Use `\\caption*`** if you want the description to be centered and styled exactly like your other captions but without a number.\n\n### Pro Tip:\nIn most scientific documents, **Table captions** go **above** the table, while **Figure captions** go **below** the figure. However, a \"Source\" or \"Note\" note is almost always placed directly below the table.",
